course: dictionary
lesson: 5372
title: A knack for words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A knack is a special skill, or a clever way of doing something that is hard to explain. Someone with a knack for drawing can make a good picture without much effort, and they may not be able to say exactly how they do it.
explain: Knack can also mean a habit, often one that happens by accident, like «a knack for getting into trouble». Check the words around it to see which meaning fits: a skill or a habit.
explain: Teachers use knack when learning is tricky. «You'll get the knack of it» means that after practice, the hard part will suddenly feel easy. Watch for the phrases have a knack for, get the knack of and there's a knack to it.
explain: «Rizz comes from "charisma" and means charm, the knack of getting people to like you.» Here knack means a skill. Try swapping in the word skill: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A knack is a special skill or a clever way of doing something, often one that is hard to explain.
fact: «A knack for getting into trouble» means a habit, often one that happens by accident.
fact: Useful phrases: have a knack for, get the knack of, there's a knack to it.
fact: «Rizz» means charm, the knack of getting people to like you; it was the Oxford Word of the Year for 2023.
fact: Knack is usually used in the singular: a knack, not knacks.
exercise: Maya has a knack for drawing. What does knack mean? | A long list | A broken tool | A wet, grey day | *A special skill
exercise: «You'll get the knack of it» is said to a child learning long division. What does it mean? | You will never understand it | You must stop trying | You need to buy a new book | *The hard part will soon feel easy with practice
exercise: «Rizz comes from "charisma" and means charm.» Which word does rizz come from? | knack | Oxford | sentence | *charisma
test: Lily says, «There's a knack to skipping rope, so keep your feet light.» What does knack mean here? | *A clever way of doing something that is hard to explain | A loud noise | A type of rope | A useful tool
test: Which phrase do teachers use when a pupil is learning something tricky? | Hand it in on Monday | Tidy up now | Go to bed early | *Get the knack of it
exam: Which is the best meaning of knack in «He has a knack for getting into trouble»? | *A habit, often one that happens by accident | A special talent for drawing | A long walk | A wet, grey day
